│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz achieves excellent results (test accuracy 0.90, generalization gap     │
│                          │ 0.01) through extreme parameter sharing with only 4 unique parameters per block. To       │
│                          │ improve further, I propose adding per-qubit phase tuning via individual RZ rotations      │
│                          │ after the collective phase layer.                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Rationale:                                                                                │
│                          │ 1. The collective approach maintains symmetry and efficiency but limits fine-grained      │
│                          │ expressivity                                                                              │
│                          │ 2. Adding 8 individual RZ parameters allows each qubit to learn its own phase offset      │
│                          │ while preserving the structured collective input/output mixers                            │
│                          │ 3. This increases expressivity (12 unique params per block vs. current 4) while remaining │
│                          │ highly parameter-efficient (74 total params vs. previous 146-194)                         │
│                          │ 4. Per-qubit phases are computationally cheap but can capture subtle qubit-specific       │
│                          │ effects from the feature map encoding                                                     │
│                          │ 5. The pattern fits naturally between the collective phase and entanglement, allowing     │
│                          │ phase information to influence the interaction structure                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected impact:                                                                          │
│                          │ - Modest increase in test accuracy (targeting 0.91-0.92)                                  │
│                          │ - Maintained small generalization gap                                                     │
│                          │ - Fast convergence due to low parameter count                                             │
│                          │ - Improved parameter efficiency through targeted expressivity                             │
